[s0l0!-header][h1]The sprintf and sscanf Functions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A formatted string can be created with the [b]sprintf()[/b] function. This is useful for building a string from other data types.
[s0l0!-header][b]
For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1747"]#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
  char info[100];
  char dept[ ] = "HR";
  int emp = 75;
  [b]sprintf[/b](info, "The %s dept has %d employees.", dept, emp);
  printf("%s\n", info);

  return 0;
}[/code]
Another useful function is [b]sscanf()[/b] for scanning a string for values. The function reads values from a string and stores them at the corresponding variable addresses. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1746"]#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
  char info[ ] = "Snoqualmie WA 13190";
  char city[50];
  char state[50];
  int population;
  [b]sscanf[/b](info, "%s %s %d", city, state, &population);
  printf("%d people live in %s, %s.", population, city, state);

  return 0;
}[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

