[s0l0!-header][h1]The string.h Library[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]string.h[/b] library contains numerous string functions. 
The statement [b]#include <string.h>[/b] at the top of your program gives you access to the following:
[b]strlen(str) [/b]Returns the length of the string stored in [b]str[/b], not including the NULL character.
[b]strcat(str1, str2) [/b]Appends (concatenates) [b]str2 [/b]to the end of [b]str1 [/b]and returns a pointer to [b]str1[/b].
[b]strcpy(str1, str2)[/b] Copies [b]str2 [/b]to [b]str1[/b]. This function is useful for assigning a string a new value.

The program below demonstrates [b]string.h[/b] functions: [code format="c" codeId="1748"]#include <stdio.h>
[b]#include <string.h>[/b]

int main()
{
  char s1[ ] = "The grey fox";
  char s2[ ] = " jumped.";
    
  [b]strcat[/b](s1, s2);
  printf("%s\n", s1);
  printf("Length of s1 is %d\n", [b]strlen[/b](s1));
  [b]strcpy[/b](s1, s2);
  printf("s1 is now %s \n", s1);

  return 0;
}[/code]
Additional[b] string.h [/b]functions include:
[b]strncat(str1, str2, n)[/b] Appends (concatenates) first [b]n[/b] characters of [b]str2 [/b]to the end of [b]str1 [/b]and returns a pointer to [b]str1[/b].
[b]strncpy(str1, str2, n)[/b] Copies the first [b]n[/b] characters of [b]str2 [/b]to [b]str1[/b].
[b]strcmp(str1, str2) [/b]Returns 0 when [b]str1 [/b]is equal to [b]str2[/b], less than 0 when [b]str1 [/b]< [b]str2[/b], and greater than 0 when [b]str1 [/b]> [b]str2[/b].
[b]strncmp(str1, str2, n)[/b] Returns 0 when the first [b]n[/b] characters of [b]str1 [/b]is equal to the first n characters of [b]str2[/b], less than 0 when [b]str1 [/b]< [b]str2[/b], and greater than 0 when [b]str1 [/b]> [b]str2[/b].
[b]strchr(str1, c)[/b] Returns a pointer to the first occurrence of char[b] c[/b] in [b]str1[/b], or NULL if character not found.
[b]strrchr(str1, c)[/b] Searches [b]str1 [/b]in reverse and returns a pointer to the position of char [b]c [/b]in [b]str1[/b], or NULL if character not found.
[b]strstr(str1, str2)[/b] Returns a pointer to the first occurrence of [b]str2 [/b]in [b]str1[/b], or NULL if [b]str2 [/b]not found.
