[s0l0!-header][h1]Converting a String to a Number[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Converting a string of number characters to a numeric value is a common task in C programming and is often used to prevent a run-time error. 
Reading a string is less error-prone than expecting a numeric value, only to have the user accidentally type an "o" rather than a "0" (zero).

The [b]stdio.h[/b] library contains the following functions for converting a string to a number:
[b]int atoi(str)[/b] Stands for ASCII to integer. Converts [b]str [/b]to the equivalent int value. 0 is returned if the first character is not a number or no numbers are encountered.
[b]double atof(str)[/b] Stands for ASCII to float. Converts [b]str [/b]to the equivalent double value. 0.0 is returned if the first character is not a number or no numbers are encountered.
[b]long int atol(str)[/b] Stands for ASCII to long int. Converts [b]str [/b]to the equivalent long integer value. 0 is returned if the first character is not a number or no numbers are encountered.

The following program demonstrates [b]atoi[/b]. [code format="c" codeId="1749"]#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
  char input[10];
  int num;
    
  printf("Enter a number: ");
  gets(input);
  num = [b]atoi[/b](input);

  return 0;
}[/code]
[note]Note, that [b]atoi()[/b] lacks error handling, and it is recommended to use [b]strtol()[/b] if you want to make sure that proper error handling is done.[/note]

