[s0l0!-header][h1]Array of Strings[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A two-dimensional array can be used to store related strings. 
Consider the following statement which declares an array with 3 elements, each holding 15 characters: [code format="c" ]char trip[3][15] = {
  "suitcase",
  "passport",
  "ticket"
};[/code]
Although the string lengths vary, it is necessary to declare a size large enough to hold the longest string. Additionally, it can be very cumbersome to access the elements. 
Referring to trip[0] for "suitcase" is error-prone. Instead, you must think of the element at [b][0][0][/b] as[b] 's'[/b], the element at [b][2][3][/b] as [b]'k'[/b], and so on.

An easier, more intuitive way to deal with a collection of related strings is with an array of pointers, as in the following program: [code format="c" codeId="1750"]char *trip[ ] = {
  "suitcase",
  "passport",
  "ticket"
};

printf("Please bring the following:\n");
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  printf("%s\n", trip[ i ]);
}[/code]
Because each element can vary in length, the array of string pointers has a more ragged structure as opposed to a two-dimensional grid structure. 
With this approach, there is no limit to the string length. And more importantly, items can be referred to by a pointer to the first character of each string.

Keep in mind that a declaration like [b]char *items[3][/b]; only reserves space for three pointers; the actual strings are being referenced by those pointers.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

